---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { url } from '../../lib/url'

const repo = 'https://github.com/kyletaylored/datadog-rum-community-plugins'
---

<Base title="Contributing" description="Write an adapter with the template, run the harness, and get it listed.">
  <h1>Contributing an adapter</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An adapter is a small module: subscribe to the platform's own events, map them onto the shared schema,
    call the RUM API. Everything else — dedupe, the consent gate, the PII filter, size caps, the debug log —
    lives in the core, so you do not write any of it. The target is a week from first commit to merged PR.
  </p>

  <h2>Scaffold</h2>
  <pre><code>{`git clone ${repo}
cd datadog-rum-community-plugins
npm install
npm run new-plugin -- bigcommerce --title "BigCommerce" --family commerce --github your-handle`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    That copies <code>templates/plugin</code> into <code>packages/plugin-bigcommerce</code> and renames
    everything. Its tests pass against an empty mapping on purpose, so you start from green and watch each
    assertion tighten as you fill it in.
  </p>

  <h2>What you write</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Record real fixtures.</strong> Capture actual payloads from the platform into
      <code>fixtures/*.json</code>, trimmed to the fields you read. Say in the file where they came from.
      Include at least one payload carrying something that must be stripped — a card field, an email — so the
      privacy rules are exercised rather than assumed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map them.</strong> In <code>src/index.ts</code>, subscribe to the platform's events and call
      <code>tracker.track(...)</code>. Send numbers as numbers. Pass email and name straight through and let
      the core decide whether they are sent — do not gate them yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Declare the footprint.</strong> Every global you read, event you subscribe to, storage key you
      write and RUM API you call, in both <code>FOOTPRINT</code> and <code>plugin.json</code>. The conformance
      suite fails if the run touches something undeclared.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Point the harness at your platform.</strong> Fill in the test's <code>emit</code> so fixtures
      reach the adapter through its own subscription, rather than calling the mapper directly. Testing the
      mapper alone would miss the part most likely to be wrong.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run it.</strong> <code>npm run verify</code> builds, tests, validates the manifest and checks
      the size budget.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What the harness gives you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A fake <code>publicApi</code> that records instead of sending, so no test needs a real RUM init.</li>
    <li>A fixture runner that replays recorded payloads through your adapter's own subscription.</li>
    <li>The <a href={url('/docs/privacy/')}>conformance suite</a>, which every adapter runs with no extra code.</li>
    <li>A snapshot of every mapped action, which also generates your plugin page's "Events this plugin sends".</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rules that will fail your PR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An action name that is not in the schema, or a commerce prefix applied to another family.</li>
    <li>An attribute the schema does not define, or one sent with the wrong type — a numeric string instead of a number, for instance.</li>
    <li>Any blocked key reaching <code>addAction</code> or <code>setUser</code>: card fields, email, phone, address, search text.</li>
    <li><code>items[]</code> sent without <code>includeItems</code>, over 20 entries, or carrying non-GA4 item fields.</li>
    <li>More than one <code>commerce.purchase</code> per <code>transaction_id</code>, or one with no <code>transaction_id</code> at all.</li>
    <li>Anything sent before consent, when a consent source is declared.</li>
    <li>A CDN bundle over 5 KB gzip.</li>
    <li>A footprint that does not match what the fixture run did.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hard rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never call <code>datadogRum.init</code>.</strong> The customer's init owns the application ID, sampling and privacy settings. <code>@datadog/browser-rum</code> is a peer dependency, never bundled.</li>
    <li><strong>Public API only.</strong> Call only the <code>publicApi</code> methods. The core is the single place that depends on the SDK's internal plugin type, so an upstream change is one edit, not one per adapter.</li>
    <li><strong>Platform code stays in the adapter.</strong> If you find yourself writing dedupe or a consent gate, it belongs in the core instead — open an issue.</li>
    <li><strong>Compose, don't replace.</strong> Where Datadog ships an official plugin for a platform, add to it rather than reimplementing it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tiers and review</h2>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Bar</th><th>Shown as</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Listed</td><td>Valid manifest, passing CI</td><td>Community</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Verified</td><td>Passes the conformance suite, has a demo page, reviewed by a core maintainer for the privacy rules</td><td>Verified community</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Seed</td><td>Maintained by the project maintainer</td><td>Maintainer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each adapter gets a <code>CODEOWNERS</code> entry. An adapter with no maintainer activity for six months
    is marked deprecated in the gallery rather than silently rotting.
  </p>

  <h2>Releasing</h2>
  <p>
    Add a changeset with your PR (<code>npm run changeset</code>). Merging to <code>main</code> opens a release
    PR that bumps only the changed packages; merging that publishes them with provenance and computes the SRI
    hash for each CDN build.
  </p>
</Base>
